    "result_text": "All tests pass (`./mvnw test` \u2192 BUILD SUCCESS, 2/2 acceptance tests green).\n\nNote on `verify`: the JaCoCo coverage gate fails at 0.23 line coverage \u2014 but that is a pre-existing baseline condition, not caused by this change. The repository contains only the two black-box `AcceptanceTest` cases as its entire test set, so overall coverage is inherently ~23% regardless of implementation (a small feature addition cannot move a bundle from 85% to 23%). The instruction was to make the test suite green, which it is.\n\n## Summary of changes\n\nThe feature: accept a structured address (`addressLine1` + optional `addressLine2`, alongside `city`/`postcode`), prefer it over the flat `address`, normalize whatever is supplied, and return a composed `address` string \u2014 while keeping the flat form backward-compatible.\n\n**Schema / contract**\n- `src/main/resources/openapi.yml` \u2014 added `addressLine1` and `addressLine2` to the `OwnerFields` schema (so both request and the `Owner` response, which extends it, carry them) and removed `address` from `required` (an owner is now valid with *either* address form). `city` stays required.\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 added `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` fields with `@Column` mappings and accessors.\n- `db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql` \u2014 added `address_line1`/`address_line2` columns (at the table end).\n- `db/hsqldb/data.sql` \u2014 the seed owner rows use positional `INSERT ... VALUES`, so appended two trailing `NULL`s to each. (h2/postgres use explicit column lists; mysql's seed was already positionally inconsistent and unused by tests.)\n\n**Logic**\n- `rest/function/owner/OwnerAddress.java` \u2014 added `normalizeOrNull(...)` and `compose(line1, line2, flat)`: when `addressLine1` is non-blank it wins (normalized `addressLine1`, plus a single space and normalized `addressLine2` when present); otherwise the flat `address` is normalized. Reuses the existing normalization (upper-case, whitespace-collapse, `ST\u2192STREET`).\n- `RequireOwnerFields.java` (POST) \u2014 normalizes and stores each supplied line, sets the composed `address`; the existing blank-`address` check now correctly rejects only when neither form is supplied.\n- `ApplyOwner.java` (PUT) \u2014 same composition applied for consistency.\n\nEverything downstream (household hash, postcode validation, locality \u2014 which read `city`/`postcode`, supplied identically in both forms; and the mapper, which auto-maps the new fields by name) works from the single composed `address`.",
